+# Deploy Fizzy on one server
+
+Fizzy's [Kamal configuration](../config/deploy.yml) runs the web app and Solid Queue background jobs in one container. It uses SQLite and local file storage, so you don't need to provision a database, separate job server, or object storage. Kamal Proxy handles HTTPS using Let's Encrypt.
+
+## Before you deploy
+
+- Have a Linux server running Docker. You need SSH access as a user who can run Docker commands.
+- Point your public hostname's DNS record at the server. Make sure TCP ports 80 and 443 are reachable from the internet for HTTPS and certificate issuance.
+- On your workstation, clone your fork, install the Ruby version in `.ruby-version`, run `bundle install`, and have Docker with Buildx running. Kamal starts a registry on your workstation and forwards it over SSH when the server pulls the image. Port 5555 must be free on both machines, and the server must allow remote SSH port forwarding for the deploy user. You don't need a registry account or to expose port 5555 publicly.
+
+## Configure and deploy
+
+1. Edit `config/deploy.yml`. Replace `fizzy.example.com` with your hostname (no `https://`). Adjust `ssh.user` and `builder.arch` to match your server if necessary.
+2. Set `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` in the same file. Use a sender your mail server permits; Fizzy uses email to send sign-in codes.
+
+   - Fizzy requires STARTTLS on port 587 by default. Set `SMTP_TLS: implicit` for TLS on port 465 or `SMTP_TLS: none` for a trusted plaintext relay. Set `SMTP_PORT` if your server uses a different port.
+   - Omit `SMTP_USERNAME` for an unauthenticated relay. If needed, set `SMTP_DOMAIN` to override the SMTP greeting domain (which defaults to your hostname) or `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` to override the default `plain` authentication method.
+3. Create your local secrets file:
+
+   ```sh
+   cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+   chmod 600 .kamal/secrets
+   bin/rails secret
+   ```
+
+   Put the generated value in `.kamal/secrets` as `SECRET_KEY_BASE`, and fill in `SMTP_PASSWORD` (leave it blank for an unauthenticated relay). Use single quotes around literal passwords containing characters like `$` or `#`. The file is ignored by Git and excluded from the Docker build. Keep the same `SECRET_KEY_BASE` across deployments so existing sessions and signed links remain valid.
+4. From the root of the checkout, run:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/kamal setup
+   ```
+
+   Once the app is healthy, open `https://<your-hostname>` and create the first account using an email address you can receive mail at. Use `bin/kamal app logs` to inspect the app and job logs if you don't receive a sign-in code.
+
+For browser push notifications, [generate a VAPID key pair](development.md#web-push-notifications) and add `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` to both `env.secret` in `config/deploy.yml` and your `.kamal/secrets` file.
+
+## Updates and data
+
+Commit changes to the app before running `bin/kamal deploy`; by default Kamal builds from the committed checkout. The entrypoint prepares and migrates the databases each time the app starts. The named Docker volume `fizzy_storage` holds all SQLite databases and uploaded files, and Kamal reuses it across deployments. Back up the volume and your secrets before upgrading, and do not delete or prune the volume if you want to keep your data.
